1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,833
I simulated a hundred hour training job on cheap spot machines that

2
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:06,603
get taken away at random.

3
00:00:06,603 --> 00:00:09,045
If the job never saves its progress,

4
00:00:09,045 --> 00:00:12,585
the expected time to finish is over two million hours.

5
00:00:12,585 --> 00:00:14,416
It effectively never ends.

6
00:00:14,766 --> 00:00:19,075
Save progress every thirty-four minutes and it finishes in about a hundred

7
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:20,124
and nine hours,

8
00:00:20,124 --> 00:00:22,279
for a third of the on-demand price.

9
00:00:22,279 --> 00:00:25,538
Save too often and it costs more than not using spot at

10
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:29,295
all. This lesson is about renting compute without wasting money.

11
00:00:30,545 --> 00:00:33,894
Welcome to lesson two of the cloud for A I engineers course,

12
00:00:33,894 --> 00:00:35,203
on The Code Notebook.

13
00:00:35,553 --> 00:00:38,687
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

14
00:00:38,687 --> 00:00:39,609
the description.

15
00:00:40,859 --> 00:00:41,772
Welcome back.

16
00:00:41,772 --> 00:00:44,942
In lesson one we learned the four numbers on a GPU spec

17
00:00:44,942 --> 00:00:47,735
sheet, and how to check whether your model fits.

18
00:00:47,735 --> 00:00:48,594
Now we shop.

19
00:00:48,594 --> 00:00:50,045
Which chip do you rent,

20
00:00:50,045 --> 00:00:51,441
how do you pay for it,

21
00:00:51,441 --> 00:00:54,396
and how do you stop paying when you do not need it?

22
00:00:54,746 --> 00:00:58,605
Prices and chip names in this lesson come from vendors and are

23
00:00:58,605 --> 00:01:00,710
dated October twenty twenty-six.

24
00:01:00,710 --> 00:01:02,932
The sums and simulations are mine,

25
00:01:02,932 --> 00:01:04,393
and I label each one.

26
00:01:04,393 --> 00:01:06,440
Chip availability changes fast,

27
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:09,597
so check the vendor page before you book anything.

28
00:01:10,847 --> 00:01:13,107
Four families of chips matter.

29
00:01:13,107 --> 00:01:16,497
N V I D I A is the default and runs everywhere.

30
00:01:16,497 --> 00:01:18,757
A M D is the main alternative.

31
00:01:18,757 --> 00:01:20,817
Google makes its own chips,

32
00:01:20,817 --> 00:01:21,881
called TPUs,

33
00:01:21,881 --> 00:01:24,539
and rents them only on Google Cloud.

34
00:01:24,539 --> 00:01:26,268
Amazon makes Trainium,

35
00:01:26,268 --> 00:01:27,929
rented only on A W S.

36
00:01:28,279 --> 00:01:29,792
Think of four car brands.

37
00:01:29,792 --> 00:01:31,670
They all drive on the same road,

38
00:01:31,670 --> 00:01:33,392
but they need different fuel,

39
00:01:33,392 --> 00:01:34,853
and different mechanics.

40
00:01:34,853 --> 00:01:36,418
The fuel here is software,

41
00:01:36,418 --> 00:01:38,713
and that is what you will pay to switch.

42
00:01:39,963 --> 00:01:41,354
Here is the map.

43
00:01:41,354 --> 00:01:44,830
N V I D I A uses a software stack called CUDA,

44
00:01:44,830 --> 00:01:47,194
and it has the widest support.

45
00:01:47,194 --> 00:01:48,794
A M D uses R O C m,

46
00:01:48,794 --> 00:01:51,088
and stock PyTorch runs on it.

47
00:01:51,438 --> 00:01:55,340
Google's TPUs work best with a framework called JAX.

48
00:01:55,340 --> 00:01:58,406
Amazon's Trainium uses the Neuron S D K.

49
00:01:58,756 --> 00:02:00,119
The pattern is simple.

50
00:02:00,119 --> 00:02:01,691
The more special the chip,

51
00:02:01,691 --> 00:02:03,054
the cheaper it can be,

52
00:02:03,054 --> 00:02:05,204
and the more you depend on one cloud.

53
00:02:05,204 --> 00:02:06,252
That is a trade,

54
00:02:06,252 --> 00:02:07,196
not a mistake.

55
00:02:07,196 --> 00:02:08,663
Just make it on purpose.

56
00:02:09,913 --> 00:02:12,131
Start with the N V I D I A names.

57
00:02:12,131 --> 00:02:15,667
The H one hundred and H two hundred are the workhorses,

58
00:02:15,667 --> 00:02:17,525
rentable almost everywhere.

59
00:02:17,525 --> 00:02:18,843
The B two hundred,

60
00:02:18,843 --> 00:02:20,881
from the Blackwell generation,

61
00:02:20,881 --> 00:02:22,739
is generally available too,

62
00:02:22,739 --> 00:02:27,294
and the B three hundred family is broadly rentable in twenty twenty-six.

63
00:02:27,644 --> 00:02:28,468
The newest,

64
00:02:28,468 --> 00:02:29,677
called Vera Rubin,

65
00:02:29,677 --> 00:02:31,656
is only in limited availability.

66
00:02:31,656 --> 00:02:35,008
One cloud began renting it on the thirtieth of September.

67
00:02:35,008 --> 00:02:37,591
If you are planning a project this quarter,

68
00:02:37,591 --> 00:02:40,229
plan around the chips you can actually book.

69
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:44,752
A M D's chip to know is the M I three fifty-five

70
00:02:44,752 --> 00:02:48,717
X. It has two hundred and eighty-eight gigabytes of memory,

71
00:02:48,717 --> 00:02:51,297
the same as the top N V I D I A tier,

72
00:02:51,297 --> 00:02:54,821
and it is generally available on a couple of clouds.

73
00:02:55,171 --> 00:02:57,367
Its software is called R O C m,

74
00:02:57,367 --> 00:02:58,182
not CUDA.

75
00:02:58,182 --> 00:03:01,821
Stock PyTorch and the popular serving tools run on it.

76
00:03:01,821 --> 00:03:05,460
The risk is custom code written specifically for CUDA,

77
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:07,029
which will need work.

78
00:03:08,279 --> 00:03:10,819
Google's newest TPU is called Ironwood.

79
00:03:10,819 --> 00:03:14,421
It became generally available in April twenty twenty-six,

80
00:03:14,421 --> 00:03:17,670
with one hundred and ninety-two gigabytes per chip.

81
00:03:17,670 --> 00:03:19,383
Its party trick is scale.

82
00:03:19,383 --> 00:03:21,568
A single pod links nine thousand,

83
00:03:21,568 --> 00:03:24,108
two hundred and sixteen chips together.

84
00:03:24,458 --> 00:03:29,337
A cheaper TPU called Trillium exists for serving and fine tuning smaller

85
00:03:29,337 --> 00:03:32,355
models. You rent TPUs only on Google Cloud,

86
00:03:32,355 --> 00:03:35,115
and the best framework for them is JAX.

87
00:03:36,365 --> 00:03:38,100
Amazon's chip is Trainium.

88
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,899
The third generation became generally available in December twenty twenty-five,

89
00:03:42,899 --> 00:03:46,079
with one hundred and forty-four gigabytes per chip.

90
00:03:46,079 --> 00:03:46,946
A big rack,

91
00:03:46,946 --> 00:03:48,449
called an UltraServer,

92
00:03:48,449 --> 00:03:51,051
links one hundred and forty-four of them.

93
00:03:51,401 --> 00:03:54,914
Amazon also makes a serving chip called Inferentia.

94
00:03:54,914 --> 00:03:57,596
You use both through the Neuron S D K,

95
00:03:57,596 --> 00:03:59,959
which works with PyTorch and JAX.

96
00:03:59,959 --> 00:04:02,450
The pitch is price for performance,

97
00:04:02,450 --> 00:04:05,069
in exchange for being tied to Amazon.

98
00:04:06,319 --> 00:04:09,524
What does switching away from N V I D I A actually

99
00:04:09,524 --> 00:04:11,126
cost? Mostly one thing.

100
00:04:11,126 --> 00:04:13,204
Custom kernels written in CUDA.

101
00:04:13,204 --> 00:04:14,925
These are small programs,

102
00:04:14,925 --> 00:04:16,171
often hand tuned,

103
00:04:16,171 --> 00:04:18,012
that some projects rely on.

104
00:04:18,362 --> 00:04:20,931
If your model is stock PyTorch or JAX,

105
00:04:20,931 --> 00:04:23,930
it moves far more easily than you might fear.

106
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:26,132
If you depend on custom kernels,

107
00:04:26,132 --> 00:04:27,846
budget engineering time.

108
00:04:27,846 --> 00:04:31,456
Always test your own workload before you sign anything.

109
00:04:32,706 --> 00:04:33,933
A word on status.

110
00:04:33,933 --> 00:04:36,680
Vendors use words like generally available,

111
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:38,025
limited, announced,

112
00:04:38,025 --> 00:04:39,661
and previous generation.

113
00:04:39,661 --> 00:04:42,701
Generally available means you can rent it today.

114
00:04:43,051 --> 00:04:44,965
Limited means a waiting list.

115
00:04:44,965 --> 00:04:46,589
Announced means a slide.

116
00:04:46,589 --> 00:04:48,213
And previous generation,

117
00:04:48,213 --> 00:04:49,721
which people call old,

118
00:04:49,721 --> 00:04:51,345
is often the best value.

119
00:04:51,345 --> 00:04:54,825
Never build a plan around a chip that is only announced.

120
00:04:56,075 --> 00:04:58,391
Clouds name machines in a code.

121
00:04:58,391 --> 00:04:59,252
On A W S,

122
00:04:59,252 --> 00:05:03,620
p five dot forty-eight x large means one whole host with eight

123
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:04,877
H one hundreds.

124
00:05:04,877 --> 00:05:06,201
On Google Cloud,

125
00:05:06,201 --> 00:05:09,841
a three dash highgpu dash eight g means eight GPUs,

126
00:05:09,841 --> 00:05:12,554
because the g count is the GPU count.

127
00:05:12,904 --> 00:05:13,745
On Azure,

128
00:05:13,745 --> 00:05:17,040
N D ninety-six I S R H one hundred v five means

129
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:18,656
eight H one hundreds,

130
00:05:18,656 --> 00:05:20,078
and the letters I,

131
00:05:20,078 --> 00:05:22,081
S and R stand for isolated,

132
00:05:22,081 --> 00:05:24,860
premium storage and R D M A networking.

133
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:28,285
Learn to read the name before you read the price.

134
00:05:29,535 --> 00:05:30,815
Where do you rent?

135
00:05:30,815 --> 00:05:32,328
Two kinds of provider.

136
00:05:32,328 --> 00:05:33,433
The big clouds,

137
00:05:33,433 --> 00:05:34,830
called hyperscalers,

138
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:36,924
and newer specialist GPU clouds,

139
00:05:36,924 --> 00:05:38,146
called neoclouds.

140
00:05:38,146 --> 00:05:42,044
Neoclouds often list the same GPU at roughly half to two thirds

141
00:05:42,044 --> 00:05:43,615
of the big cloud price,

142
00:05:43,615 --> 00:05:46,000
and they tend to get new chips first.

143
00:05:46,350 --> 00:05:49,373
The big clouds win on the things around the GPU.

144
00:05:49,373 --> 00:05:51,176
Your data is already there,

145
00:05:51,176 --> 00:05:53,560
your identity and network are set up,

146
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:56,816
and they offer managed services and their own chips.

147
00:05:56,816 --> 00:05:58,793
A common answer is to do both.

148
00:06:00,043 --> 00:06:01,102
Now how to pay.

149
00:06:01,102 --> 00:06:02,328
There is a ladder,

150
00:06:02,328 --> 00:06:05,560
and every step down is cheaper and comes with strings.

151
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,343
On demand is the base price,

152
00:06:07,343 --> 00:06:08,234
no promises.

153
00:06:08,584 --> 00:06:09,649
Queued capacity,

154
00:06:09,649 --> 00:06:10,820
called flex start,

155
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:12,363
can be around half price,

156
00:06:12,363 --> 00:06:14,173
but your job waits for a slot.

157
00:06:14,173 --> 00:06:18,005
A one to three year commitment goes down to about forty-five percent

158
00:06:18,005 --> 00:06:18,910
of the price,

159
00:06:18,910 --> 00:06:19,868
if you use it.

160
00:06:20,218 --> 00:06:21,570
Spot is the cheapest,

161
00:06:21,570 --> 00:06:23,246
down to about nine percent,

162
00:06:23,246 --> 00:06:26,003
because the provider can take the machine back.

163
00:06:26,003 --> 00:06:28,220
These are the vendors' up to figures,

164
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:29,733
and real discounts vary.

165
00:06:30,983 --> 00:06:34,436
A commitment means you promise to pay for a GPU for one

166
00:06:34,436 --> 00:06:35,548
to three years,

167
00:06:35,548 --> 00:06:39,235
in return for a discount of up to about fifty-five percent.

168
00:06:39,235 --> 00:06:43,039
It is the right tool for a steady workload that never sleeps,

169
00:06:43,039 --> 00:06:45,029
like a live support assistant.

170
00:06:45,379 --> 00:06:47,398
There are also prepaid windows,

171
00:06:47,398 --> 00:06:49,358
such as A W S Capacity Blocks,

172
00:06:49,358 --> 00:06:52,530
where you reserve a stretch of time for a deadline,

173
00:06:52,530 --> 00:06:54,433
like a two week training run.

174
00:06:54,433 --> 00:06:55,240
Same idea,

175
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:56,394
shorter promise.

176
00:06:57,644 --> 00:06:58,861
Here is the trap,

177
00:06:58,861 --> 00:07:00,021
and it is a sum.

178
00:07:00,021 --> 00:07:03,325
You pay for a commitment even when the GPU sits idle.

179
00:07:03,325 --> 00:07:06,630
So the real price is the rate divided by how busy you

180
00:07:06,630 --> 00:07:07,325
keep it.

181
00:07:07,325 --> 00:07:10,398
Assume an on demand price of ten dollars an hour.

182
00:07:10,398 --> 00:07:11,905
That is my assumption,

183
00:07:11,905 --> 00:07:13,529
to keep the maths clean.

184
00:07:13,878 --> 00:07:17,057
A fifty-five percent commitment costs four fifty.

185
00:07:17,057 --> 00:07:18,677
At ninety percent busy,

186
00:07:18,677 --> 00:07:21,616
the real price is five dollars per used hour.

187
00:07:21,616 --> 00:07:23,475
At forty-five percent busy,

188
00:07:23,475 --> 00:07:24,315
it is ten,

189
00:07:24,315 --> 00:07:25,874
the same as on demand.

190
00:07:26,224 --> 00:07:27,711
At thirty percent busy,

191
00:07:27,711 --> 00:07:28,812
fifteen dollars.

192
00:07:28,812 --> 00:07:29,858
At ten percent,

193
00:07:29,858 --> 00:07:32,667
forty-five. The discount turned into a penalty.

194
00:07:33,917 --> 00:07:35,342
So here is the rule.

195
00:07:35,342 --> 00:07:39,560
A commitment with a discount of fifty-five percent only pays off if

196
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:43,540
you keep the GPU busy more than forty-five percent of the time.

197
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:45,619
That is one minus the discount.

198
00:07:45,969 --> 00:07:47,226
If you are not sure,

199
00:07:47,226 --> 00:07:48,273
start on demand,

200
00:07:48,273 --> 00:07:50,577
measure your real busy time for a month,

201
00:07:50,577 --> 00:07:52,881
and then commit to only the steady part.

202
00:07:52,881 --> 00:07:54,452
Never commit to your peak.

203
00:07:55,702 --> 00:07:57,928
A middle option is queued capacity.

204
00:07:57,928 --> 00:08:00,211
Google calls its version Flex start,

205
00:08:00,211 --> 00:08:03,521
and it can be around fifty percent off for some chips.

206
00:08:03,521 --> 00:08:04,948
You ask for machines,

207
00:08:04,948 --> 00:08:07,402
and they arrive when capacity frees up.

208
00:08:07,752 --> 00:08:10,211
That is fine for jobs that can wait,

209
00:08:10,211 --> 00:08:12,977
like a nightly evaluation or a batch run.

210
00:08:12,977 --> 00:08:16,542
It is a bad fit for anything a customer is waiting on.

211
00:08:16,542 --> 00:08:19,307
Match the plan to how patient the job is.

212
00:08:20,557 --> 00:08:21,310
Now spot,

213
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:22,758
the biggest discount.

214
00:08:22,758 --> 00:08:24,901
Spot machines are spare capacity.

215
00:08:24,901 --> 00:08:27,797
They cost up to about ninety-one percent less,

216
00:08:27,797 --> 00:08:30,113
and the provider can take them back.

217
00:08:30,113 --> 00:08:32,661
On A W S you get two minutes of warning.

218
00:08:32,661 --> 00:08:33,414
On Azure,

219
00:08:33,414 --> 00:08:34,515
thirty seconds.

220
00:08:34,865 --> 00:08:36,169
That sounds scary.

221
00:08:36,169 --> 00:08:39,490
It is fine if your job saves its progress regularly.

222
00:08:39,490 --> 00:08:42,218
So the real question is how often to save.

223
00:08:42,218 --> 00:08:43,937
I simulated exactly that,

224
00:08:43,937 --> 00:08:45,301
so let me show you.

225
00:08:46,551 --> 00:08:47,376
The setup.

226
00:08:47,376 --> 00:08:49,733
A job needs a hundred hours of work.

227
00:08:49,733 --> 00:08:52,385
The spot machine is taken away at random,

228
00:08:52,385 --> 00:08:54,329
on average every eight hours.

229
00:08:54,329 --> 00:08:56,156
That rate is my assumption.

230
00:08:56,156 --> 00:08:57,806
After each interruption,

231
00:08:57,806 --> 00:09:00,399
I lose all the work since the last save,

232
00:09:00,399 --> 00:09:02,520
and wait six minutes to restart.

233
00:09:02,870 --> 00:09:05,896
A save costs seventy-two seconds of work time.

234
00:09:05,896 --> 00:09:08,136
I tried different save intervals,

235
00:09:08,136 --> 00:09:10,859
ran two thousand simulated jobs for each,

236
00:09:10,859 --> 00:09:15,217
and priced the result with an assumed seventy percent spot discount.

237
00:09:15,217 --> 00:09:16,730
This is a simulation,

238
00:09:16,730 --> 00:09:18,061
not real machines.

239
00:09:19,311 --> 00:09:20,277
First result.

240
00:09:20,277 --> 00:09:23,744
A job that never saves until the end needs eight hours of

241
00:09:23,744 --> 00:09:26,188
luck, twelve and a half times in a row.

242
00:09:26,188 --> 00:09:28,689
The average finish time was two million,

243
00:09:28,689 --> 00:09:30,678
two hundred and three thousand,

244
00:09:30,678 --> 00:09:32,837
five hundred and sixty-four hours.

245
00:09:33,187 --> 00:09:34,551
That is not a typo.

246
00:09:34,551 --> 00:09:37,457
A hundred hour job on interruptible machines,

247
00:09:37,457 --> 00:09:38,584
with no saving,

248
00:09:38,584 --> 00:09:40,422
effectively never finishes.

249
00:09:40,422 --> 00:09:42,913
Spot without checkpoints is not cheap.

250
00:09:42,913 --> 00:09:43,921
It is broken.

251
00:09:45,171 --> 00:09:46,148
Now add saves.

252
00:09:46,148 --> 00:09:47,341
Every eight hours,

253
00:09:47,341 --> 00:09:50,054
the job takes a hundred and seventy-two hours.

254
00:09:50,054 --> 00:09:51,139
Every two hours,

255
00:09:51,139 --> 00:09:52,549
a hundred and sixteen.

256
00:09:52,549 --> 00:09:53,905
Every thirty minutes,

257
00:09:53,905 --> 00:09:55,153
a hundred and nine.

258
00:09:55,503 --> 00:09:58,621
Save every ten minutes and it is a hundred and fifteen.

259
00:09:58,621 --> 00:09:59,784
Every two minutes,

260
00:09:59,784 --> 00:10:01,370
a hundred and sixty-three.

261
00:10:01,370 --> 00:10:02,691
Every thirty seconds,

262
00:10:02,691 --> 00:10:04,435
three hundred and forty-five.

263
00:10:04,785 --> 00:10:05,496
A U shape.

264
00:10:05,496 --> 00:10:07,526
Too rare and you lose a lot of work.

265
00:10:07,526 --> 00:10:09,810
Too often and you spend your time saving.

266
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:12,011
Now the bill.

267
00:10:12,011 --> 00:10:14,359
Priced at thirty percent of on demand,

268
00:10:14,359 --> 00:10:17,658
a save every thirty minutes costs thirty-two point six,

269
00:10:17,658 --> 00:10:19,447
against a hundred on demand.

270
00:10:19,447 --> 00:10:20,845
A third of the price.

271
00:10:21,195 --> 00:10:24,092
Every two minutes it is forty-eight point eight.

272
00:10:24,092 --> 00:10:27,547
Every thirty seconds it is a hundred and three point five.

273
00:10:27,547 --> 00:10:30,778
Saving too often cost more than not using spot at all.

274
00:10:31,128 --> 00:10:34,526
The cheapest rental in the world is the most expensive one if

275
00:10:34,526 --> 00:10:35,781
you waste its hours.

276
00:10:37,031 --> 00:10:39,805
There is a simple rule to find the sweet spot.

277
00:10:39,805 --> 00:10:44,132
Save every square root of two times the average time between interruptions

278
00:10:44,132 --> 00:10:45,852
times the cost of one save.

279
00:10:45,852 --> 00:10:46,962
With my numbers,

280
00:10:46,962 --> 00:10:49,569
that is two times eight times point oh two,

281
00:10:49,569 --> 00:10:50,956
then the square root.

282
00:10:50,956 --> 00:10:52,620
About thirty-four minutes.

283
00:10:52,970 --> 00:10:54,790
That matches the simulation,

284
00:10:54,790 --> 00:10:57,406
where thirty and thirty-four minutes tied.

285
00:10:57,406 --> 00:11:01,273
Cheap saves and frequent interruptions mean you save more often.

286
00:11:01,273 --> 00:11:04,628
Expensive saves and rare interruptions mean less often.

287
00:11:05,878 --> 00:11:07,439
So when do you use spot?

288
00:11:07,439 --> 00:11:09,892
For work that can be paused and resumed.

289
00:11:09,892 --> 00:11:11,230
Training with saves,

290
00:11:11,230 --> 00:11:12,400
batch evaluation,

291
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:13,515
data processing.

292
00:11:13,515 --> 00:11:17,139
Pay attention to the warning notice and save when it arrives.

293
00:11:17,489 --> 00:11:20,491
Do not run your only live chat endpoint on spot.

294
00:11:20,491 --> 00:11:21,415
If you must,

295
00:11:21,415 --> 00:11:24,994
keep a few on demand machines behind it so users never see

296
00:11:24,994 --> 00:11:25,802
an outage.

297
00:11:27,052 --> 00:11:29,275
How do you compare different chips?

298
00:11:29,275 --> 00:11:32,695
Divide the hourly price by the arithmetic the chip does,

299
00:11:32,695 --> 00:11:33,664
in petaflops.

300
00:11:33,664 --> 00:11:36,456
This uses vendor list prices on Google Cloud,

301
00:11:36,456 --> 00:11:38,052
from October fourteenth.

302
00:11:38,402 --> 00:11:42,835
Ironwood on demand is about two dollars sixty-one per petaflop hour.

303
00:11:42,835 --> 00:11:46,466
A B two hundred through Flex start is one seventy-nine.

304
00:11:46,466 --> 00:11:50,652
Ironwood with Flex or a three year commit is about one seventeen

305
00:11:50,652 --> 00:11:51,760
to one thirty.

306
00:11:52,110 --> 00:11:55,405
The chip with the lowest price per hour is not always the

307
00:11:55,405 --> 00:11:57,026
cheapest per unit of work.

308
00:11:57,026 --> 00:11:57,944
Divide first.

309
00:11:59,194 --> 00:12:00,774
Now stopping the meter.

310
00:12:00,774 --> 00:12:05,512
Autoscaling means adding machines when demand rises and removing them when it

311
00:12:05,512 --> 00:12:07,501
falls. It works at two levels.

312
00:12:07,851 --> 00:12:10,615
Pods are the running copies of your service.

313
00:12:10,615 --> 00:12:13,840
A tool called KEDA adds pods when requests queue up.

314
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:15,913
If the pods have nowhere to run,

315
00:12:15,913 --> 00:12:17,525
a tool called Karpenter,

316
00:12:17,525 --> 00:12:19,252
or the Cluster Autoscaler,

317
00:12:19,252 --> 00:12:20,519
adds new machines.

318
00:12:20,869 --> 00:12:23,469
Adding a GPU machine can take minutes.

319
00:12:23,469 --> 00:12:26,070
So scale on a signal that moves early,

320
00:12:26,070 --> 00:12:27,679
like the queue length,

321
00:12:27,679 --> 00:12:30,094
not on how busy the GPU already is.

322
00:12:31,344 --> 00:12:34,307
The cheapest GPU is one that is switched off.

323
00:12:34,307 --> 00:12:38,662
Scale to zero means shutting down every machine when nobody is using

324
00:12:38,662 --> 00:12:39,630
the service.

325
00:12:39,630 --> 00:12:42,412
You stop paying overnight and at weekends.

326
00:12:42,762 --> 00:12:44,624
The price is the cold start.

327
00:12:44,624 --> 00:12:48,523
The first request after a quiet spell must wait while a machine

328
00:12:48,523 --> 00:12:50,327
starts and loads the model.

329
00:12:50,327 --> 00:12:53,528
For real models that is tens of seconds to minutes.

330
00:12:53,528 --> 00:12:55,740
I simulated it with sixty seconds.

331
00:12:56,990 --> 00:13:00,108
The simulated day has a three hour busy window,

332
00:13:00,108 --> 00:13:01,821
a one hour light window,

333
00:13:01,821 --> 00:13:03,411
and silence otherwise.

334
00:13:03,411 --> 00:13:05,979
Always on bills twenty-four GPU hours.

335
00:13:05,979 --> 00:13:07,080
Scale to zero,

336
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:09,281
with a five minute idle timeout,

337
00:13:09,281 --> 00:13:11,055
bills four point oh four.

338
00:13:11,055 --> 00:13:12,889
Eighty-three percent less.

339
00:13:13,239 --> 00:13:17,035
The average wait went from one point seven seconds to two point

340
00:13:17,035 --> 00:13:21,058
three. The slowest five percent went from seven point nine to eight

341
00:13:21,058 --> 00:13:21,908
point nine.

342
00:13:22,258 --> 00:13:25,326
But there were three point nine cold starts a day,

343
00:13:25,326 --> 00:13:28,508
and each of those users waited a full sixty seconds.

344
00:13:28,508 --> 00:13:29,701
Cheap on average,

345
00:13:29,701 --> 00:13:31,519
painful for the unlucky few.

346
00:13:32,769 --> 00:13:34,621
Here are the same numbers as a trade.

347
00:13:34,971 --> 00:13:36,644
Waits barely moved on average.

348
00:13:36,994 --> 00:13:38,812
But the cold starts are real.

349
00:13:38,812 --> 00:13:40,300
If a person is waiting,

350
00:13:40,300 --> 00:13:41,787
sixty seconds is a lot.

351
00:13:43,037 --> 00:13:45,046
How do you soften a cold start?

352
00:13:45,046 --> 00:13:48,088
Keep the model files cached close to the machine,

353
00:13:48,088 --> 00:13:50,556
so you do not download them every time.

354
00:13:50,556 --> 00:13:52,163
Save compiled artifacts,

355
00:13:52,163 --> 00:13:54,229
so the model does not recompile.

356
00:13:54,229 --> 00:13:58,246
Some platforms can snapshot a loaded model and restore it quickly.

357
00:13:58,596 --> 00:14:01,747
Or keep one warm machine and scale the rest to zero.

358
00:14:01,747 --> 00:14:03,885
I am teaching these as ideas here.

359
00:14:03,885 --> 00:14:06,192
They are not measured in this lesson.

360
00:14:06,192 --> 00:14:09,118
The next lesson covers the container side of it.

361
00:14:10,368 --> 00:14:12,534
Now apply it to ShopNest Copilot.

362
00:14:12,534 --> 00:14:15,109
The live support assistant never sleeps,

363
00:14:15,109 --> 00:14:16,807
so it earns a commitment,

364
00:14:16,807 --> 00:14:18,738
but only for the steady part,

365
00:14:18,738 --> 00:14:20,436
with on demand for peaks.

366
00:14:20,436 --> 00:14:23,187
The nightly evaluation run is patient work,

367
00:14:23,187 --> 00:14:26,465
so it goes on spot with saves about every half hour.

368
00:14:26,815 --> 00:14:28,461
And the staging environment,

369
00:14:28,461 --> 00:14:29,849
used a few hours a day,

370
00:14:29,849 --> 00:14:30,826
scales to zero.

371
00:14:30,826 --> 00:14:31,854
Three workloads,

372
00:14:31,854 --> 00:14:33,911
three different rungs of the ladder.

373
00:14:33,911 --> 00:14:35,042
That is the skill.

374
00:14:36,292 --> 00:14:38,250
Four words to carry forward.

375
00:14:38,250 --> 00:14:41,797
Commitment: a promise to pay in return for a discount.

376
00:14:41,797 --> 00:14:44,122
Spot: spare capacity you can lose.

377
00:14:44,472 --> 00:14:47,015
Checkpoint: a saved copy of your progress.

378
00:14:47,015 --> 00:14:48,066
And cold start:

379
00:14:48,066 --> 00:14:50,941
the wait when a machine must start from nothing.

380
00:14:50,941 --> 00:14:53,872
You will hear all four again in the next lessons.

381
00:14:55,122 --> 00:14:56,385
Interview corner.

382
00:14:56,385 --> 00:14:58,791
Asked when a commitment is worth it,

383
00:14:58,791 --> 00:15:02,280
say: only if you keep the GPU busy more than one minus

384
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:03,302
the discount,

385
00:15:03,302 --> 00:15:07,272
so a fifty-five percent discount needs forty-five percent use.

386
00:15:07,272 --> 00:15:11,363
Otherwise the real price per used hour is higher than on demand.

387
00:15:11,713 --> 00:15:13,496
Asked how to use spot safely,

388
00:15:13,496 --> 00:15:15,118
say: checkpoint regularly,

389
00:15:15,118 --> 00:15:16,739
handle the warning notice,

390
00:15:16,739 --> 00:15:19,549
and pick the interval with the square root rule,

391
00:15:19,549 --> 00:15:22,684
which gave about thirty-four minutes in my simulation.

392
00:15:23,934 --> 00:15:24,709
Your turn.

393
00:15:24,709 --> 00:15:27,254
The zip for this lesson has three scripts.

394
00:15:27,254 --> 00:15:29,633
The first simulates spot interruptions.

395
00:15:29,633 --> 00:15:32,400
Change the average time between interruptions,

396
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:33,894
and the cost of a save,

397
00:15:33,894 --> 00:15:35,720
and find your own sweet spot.

398
00:15:36,070 --> 00:15:38,092
The second does the buying maths,

399
00:15:38,092 --> 00:15:40,388
and the third simulates scale to zero.

400
00:15:40,388 --> 00:15:41,919
Put in your own traffic.

401
00:15:41,919 --> 00:15:45,199
One changed number teaches more than an hour of reading.

402
00:15:46,449 --> 00:15:48,979
Recap. Pick the chip for your software,

403
00:15:48,979 --> 00:15:50,274
not just the spec.

404
00:15:50,274 --> 00:15:55,041
A commitment only pays off above forty-five percent use at fifty-five percent

405
00:15:55,041 --> 00:15:58,866
off. Spot is brilliant with saves every thirty or so minutes,

406
00:15:58,866 --> 00:16:00,514
and broken without them.

407
00:16:00,864 --> 00:16:05,055
Scale to zero cut a simulated day from twenty-four GPU hours to

408
00:16:05,055 --> 00:16:08,246
four, at the price of sixty second cold starts.

409
00:16:08,246 --> 00:16:09,247
Next lesson:

410
00:16:09,247 --> 00:16:11,124
containers and Kubernetes.

411
00:16:11,124 --> 00:16:14,753
How to package a model so it runs the same everywhere.

412
00:16:16,003 --> 00:16:17,237
In the next lesson:

413
00:16:17,237 --> 00:16:18,846
containers and Kubernetes.

414
00:16:18,846 --> 00:16:21,958
How to package a model so it runs the same everywhere.

415
00:16:22,308 --> 00:16:23,900
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

416
00:16:23,900 --> 00:16:25,174
See you in the notebook.

